{
	"_comment": "Romanian. Suffixed definites (Danish/Swedish/Bulgarian family): definite_suffix by gender welds -ul (m) / strip-ă+a (f) onto the singular; definite_suffix_plural welds -le onto a known plural. Both rules were verified noun-by-noun, not assumed. Grammatically NEUTER nouns (fișier, mesaj, pachet, server, folder, element, test, depozit) are tagged gender 'm' because Romanian neuter singular declension — indefinite un, definite -ul, participle agreement — is IDENTICAL to masculine; the m/n split only matters in the plural, which this schema's single non-gendered plural-suffix rule does not need to distinguish. Nouns the given rules cannot derive were excluded rather than forced: feminine nouns ending in -e/-ie (eroare, versiune, categorie, vulnerabilitate) take +a WITHOUT stripping, which the strip:'ă' rule cannot express, so a genuinely rule-compatible synonym was substituted where one exists (greșeală for error) and dropped otherwise (version, category, vulnerability); 'child' (copil) is excluded because its plural definite is copiii (agent-noun -i class), not the -le this schema encodes — and copiile is a REAL word but means 'the copies' (copie/copii), a false-friend trap worth avoiding outright. 'push' and 'commit' render their nearest general Romanian sense (împinge = to shove/propel; înregistra = to record) rather than an assumed git-specific coinage, since Romanian dev usage of these two varies and no single standardised term is certain. The counting particle 'de' before nouns for numbers >= 20 (douăzeci de fișiere) is not modelled — the engine has no slot for it. Nominative-only; no case declension.",
